lisa-design:
- Production executable writes go through internal/agent/internal/execfile (standard library only, so pipeline, runbundle, envrun, host, katahelper, kata and codex import it without a cycle); exectest.Write is a thin test wrapper over execfile.WriteFile.
- execfile locks only when perm has an exec bit (any of 0o111): a 0600 file is never exec'd, and the lock would only hold up forks. So callers that write mixed modes (skills.go, runbundle.writeFile, codex copyFile, host Extract) call it for every file and the mode decides.
- The lock is held per file from open to close (the fill callback), never across a tree walk; reads that precede the open (skills.go's readLimited, runbundle's MkdirAll) stay outside it. fill must not start a process or take ForkLock (deadlock); documented on OpenFile.
- Rule: every production file written with an exec bit goes through execfile, including the ones nothing on this host execs directly (envrun's scripts run as `bash <file>`, kataprivileges' staged helper is copied by install, provision's and katahelper's build-context scripts, kata's agent copy), so the rule is checkable by grep rather than by tracing each exec.
- runbundle's TestImports allow-list gains execfile (stdlib only, no network), and workspace-run.md's import line says so.

